# Knight's permission lists — ONE definition, used by the runner and by test-guards.sh.
#
# ── THESE ARE ARRAYS, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE ─────────────────────────
# They were plain strings, expanded unquoted as `--disallowedTools $KNIGHT_DENY`. Every pattern
# containing a space was silently shredded by word-splitting:
#
#     Bash(git push:*)   →   "Bash(git"   "push:*)"
#
# Two meaningless arguments. So `Bash(git push:*)` was never a working deny rule, and neither was
# any other multi-word pattern. Worse, test-guards.sh "verified" the list by grepping the STRING for
# that pattern — it was present, so the check passed, while nothing enforced it at runtime.
#
# That is the exact failure this whole system keeps meeting: a check that validates the DECLARATION
# instead of the BEHAVIOR. The suite now runs a live `git push` under the real list instead.
#
# Arrays preserve each pattern as one argument. Always expand them as "${KNIGHT_DENY[@]}" — quoted,
# with the [@] — and never flatten one back into a string.
#
# ── WHAT ACTUALLY PROTECTS KNIGHT ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not these lists. Two structural facts, each verified with recorded permission denials:
#   * THE SANDBOX — Claude Code confines file access to the working directory, across Write, Edit
#     and Bash, for reads as well as writes. Knight's cwd is his own clone, so ~/.claude, Brad's
#     checkout and /etc are unreachable. Never pass --add-dir; that is what would open it.
#   * NO GIT REMOTE — the clone has none, so pushing has nowhere to go. Mechanical, not advisory.
# The lists below are defense in depth. Withholding a single tool proved unreliable in testing (the
# model substituted another tool to reach the same effect), so guards constrain EFFECTS, not names.

# Permitted without a human to approve. --permission-mode acceptEdits auto-approves file edits but
# NOT Bash, so a headless run has nobody to approve `npx vitest` and it is refused — Knight's first
# real task wrote tests then failed four times trying to RUN them. A short list of verbs, not a
# blanket Bash(*). Anything absent is not forbidden, it simply stops and asks, which headless means
# it does not happen: a missing allow costs a retry, a missing deny costs damage.
KNIGHT_ALLOW=(
  "Bash(npx vitest:*)" "Bash(npx tsc:*)" "Bash(npm run:*)" "Bash(npm test:*)"
  "Bash(git add:*)" "Bash(git commit:*)" "Bash(git status:*)" "Bash(git diff:*)"
  "Bash(git log:*)" "Bash(git show:*)" "Bash(git restore:*)" "Bash(git checkout:*)"
  "Bash(ls:*)" "Bash(cat:*)" "Bash(head:*)" "Bash(tail:*)"
  "Bash(grep:*)" "Bash(rg:*)" "Bash(find:*)" "Bash(wc:*)"
  # The Python targets' gate is `.venv/bin/python <file>_test.py`, and without this line the agent
  # cannot run it — every attempt stops to ask a human who is not there. 2026-08-23: a job on the
  # moses target wrote a test it was never able to execute once, which is the same shape of failure
  # as a gate nobody has watched go red. Scoped to the interpreter INSIDE the clone (the one KT_PREP
  # builds) rather than python3, so it exists only where the sandbox already confines it.
  "Bash(.venv/bin/python:*)"
)

# Deny beats allow, so `git push` stays impossible even though `git` verbs are permitted above.
KNIGHT_DENY=(
  "Bash(sudo:*)" "Bash(git push:*)" "Bash(git remote:*)" "Bash(systemctl:*)"
  "Bash(rm:*)" "Bash(shutdown:*)" "Bash(reboot:*)" "Bash(mkfs:*)" "Bash(dd:*)"
  "Bash(mount:*)" "Bash(umount:*)" "Bash(crontab:*)" "Bash(chown:*)" "Bash(chmod:*)"
  "Bash(curl:*)" "Bash(wget:*)" "Bash(ssh:*)" "Bash(claude:*)"
  # The operator's home, whoever they are: knight-run has loaded moses-env.sh, and the runner hands
  # MOSES_HOME to the shell that reads this file. $HOME is the fallback for anything else.
  "Edit(${MOSES_HOME:-$HOME}/.claude/**)" "Write(${MOSES_HOME:-$HOME}/.claude/**)"
  "Read(${MOSES_HOME:-$HOME}/.claude/.credentials.json)" "Read(${MOSES_HOME:-$HOME}/.config/moses/**)"
  "Edit(/etc/**)" "Write(/etc/**)" "Edit(/usr/local/bin/**)" "Write(/usr/local/bin/**)"
  # NO SUB-AGENTS. Atlas's third rule, adopted 2026-09-02: parallel work is PROPOSED, never launched.
  # Knight is one clone and one worker by construction (KNIGHT_MAX_CONCURRENT=1), but that bounds
  # JOBS, not what a single job spawns from inside itself — an agent that fans out into five more is
  # five unreviewed workers on the same checkout, and the wall-clock ceiling is the only thing that
  # would ever have noticed. Denied by name rather than left to the brief, because the brief is
  # prose and prose is the weakest layer.
  "Task" "Agent"
)

# Ceilings. Wall-clock is the one that actually stops a runaway; the rest is bookkeeping.
KNIGHT_TIMEOUT_SEC="${KNIGHT_TIMEOUT_SEC:-3600}"   # 60 min — Opus at high effort on a real feature needs the room
# Overridable by env so the test suite does not spend the real allowance. The suite runs seven jobs
# with a FAKE agent — no model call, no tokens — and on 2026-08-23 running it three times exhausted
# the day's eight and made three tests fail for a reason that had nothing to do with correctness.
# A guard that fires on correct work gets switched off, and a switched-off guard protects nothing.
# The ceiling itself is still pinned: test-runner.sh sets it to 0 and asserts a start is refused.
KNIGHT_MAX_PER_DAY="${KNIGHT_MAX_PER_DAY:-8}"   # Knight spends Brad's Pro usage, shared with Brad's own sessions
KNIGHT_MAX_CONCURRENT=1        # one clone, one worker

# ── The review pass (Atlas's rules 1 and 2, adopted 2026-09-02) ───────────────
# A SEPARATE agent from the builder, spawned by the RUNNER. Atlas's own estate has the reviewer
# separated but the DECISION TO SPAWN one sitting with the author — which he named as the same shape
# as reviewing your own diff, moved up a level. So this is a fixed step in knight-run, not something
# the brief asks the builder to do.
#
# Cheaper than the build on purpose: reading a bounded diff is not the same job as writing the
# feature, and the review doubles the model calls per job against a shared daily allowance.
KNIGHT_REVIEW_MODEL="${KNIGHT_REVIEW_MODEL:-claude-sonnet-5}"
KNIGHT_REVIEW_EFFORT="${KNIGHT_REVIEW_EFFORT:-medium}"
KNIGHT_REVIEW_TIMEOUT_SEC="${KNIGHT_REVIEW_TIMEOUT_SEC:-600}"
# Read-only. The reviewer must not be able to "fix" what it finds — a reviewer that edits is an
# author, and then nobody has reviewed the edit.
KNIGHT_REVIEW_ALLOW=( "Read" "Grep" "Glob" "Bash(git diff:*)" "Bash(git log:*)" "Bash(git show:*)" )
KNIGHT_REVIEW_DENY=( "Edit" "Write" "MultiEdit" "NotebookEdit" "Task" "Agent" "WebFetch" "WebSearch"
                     "Bash(git push:*)" "Bash(git commit:*)" "Bash(sudo:*)" "Bash(rm:*)" )

# Latest Opus, high effort — Brad, 2026-08-08: "I've trusted Claude Code Opus thus far, no reason
# to stop now." The `opus` ALIAS rather than a pinned id, so "latest" stays true without an edit
# every release; it resolves to claude-opus-5 today.
KNIGHT_MODEL="opus"
KNIGHT_EFFORT="high"

# Where Knight's work goes when the gate is green. Push is done BY THE RUNNER, never by the agent:
# `Bash(git push:*)` stays denied above, and the remote is added only after the tests pass and
# removed immediately after. So during the entire window the agent is running, the clone still has
# no remote — which was the original guarantee and is the window where it matters.
# Use Brad's origin URL EXACTLY AS WRITTEN. It is SSH (git@github.com:...), and the key on Reserve
# is what authenticates. The first version rewrote it to https://github.com/owner/repo, which has no
# credentials here — no gh, no credential helper, no stored token — so every fetch died with
# "could not read Username for 'https://github.com'". It was never going to work, and the
# end-to-end proof missed it entirely because that test pushed to a LOCAL BARE REPO, which needs no
# authentication at all. Proving the sequence is not proving the connection.
# MOVED TO THE REGISTRY (2026-08-23). Push remote, branch and landing mode are now per-repository, in
# targets/<name>.env, because Knight works on more than one repo and they do not land the same way:
# Viatica goes straight to master and deploys; the repositories that run Knight go to a branch and
# wait for a human. Two globals could not express that, and a global that is right for one target and
# silently wrong for another is worse than no default at all.
#
# Deliberately not left here as a fallback. A stale global that still resolves is exactly how a push
# ends up somewhere nobody chose; if a target file is missing a field, the loader refuses by name.

# WHICH BUILT-IN TOOLS EXIST for each agent. The allow and deny lists above are PERMISSIONS: a tool
# named in neither is still present in the turn. Jon found it on 2026-09-12 (a deny list lost to
# Monitor). Measured here on 2026-09-13 from the CLI's opening event: the builder was handed 27
# built-ins and the reviewer 24, including Monitor, RemoteTrigger, CronCreate and SendMessage, and
# for the builder WebFetch and WebSearch, which are ways off a machine the sandbox otherwise closes.
# --tools is the inventory; these are what each job needs and nothing else.
KNIGHT_TOOLS="Bash,Read,Edit,Write,Glob,Grep"
KNIGHT_REVIEW_TOOLS="Read,Grep,Glob,Bash"

# THE CLI KNIGHT AND ZRYACHIY RUN, by absolute path. Installed per-user on 2026-09-13 (2.1.270) so it
# updates without root; the global /usr/bin/claude sat at 2.1.220, too old for --restricted. A bare
# `claude` resolved through whatever PATH the caller had, which for the Moses services is the old one.
KNIGHT_CLI="${MOSES_CLAUDE_CLI:-${MOSES_HOME:-$HOME}/.local/bin/claude}"   # found by moses-env.sh

# --restricted (CLI 2.1.248+) is passed to both, alongside --tools. Proven on 2026-09-13 in a throwaway
# repo with these exact settings:
# - the builder still creates files and commits;
# - a Read OUTSIDE the working folder is now refused (it was not confined before; the sandbox only
#   covered Bash);
# - naming a folder with --add-dir opens that one folder.
# The reviewer gets --add-dir for its own job folder, because review.diff lives there, outside the clone.

